WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:12.916 --> 00:00:28.292
[MUSIC - STEVIE WONDER, "PASTIME
 PARADISE"]

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:33.410
VEST: I'd like to begin with
a very personal anecdote.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:38.260
When I was quite young, probably
in first or second grade

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.544
or so, we had what,
for me growing up

00:00:42.544 --> 00:00:43.960
in a little town
in West Virginia,

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.880
was a great adventure.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.850
My parents took my brother
and me to Washington,

00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:52.270
our nation's capital.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.470
We went on the bus.

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:56.590
It was a long overnight trip.

00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:59.180
They knew I would be
so excited about this,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:01.640
they didn't tell me until
the morning we left.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.880
And it was really
quite a highlight

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:07.900
of my life to get out and see
the big, exciting city that's

00:01:07.900 --> 00:01:08.815
our nation's capital.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.310
On the way, we drove
through a little town

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.920
somewhere in Maryland.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:22.810
And the bus pulled up to a
restaurant to stop and let

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.480
people get something to eat.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:30.070
There was a young black man who,
I suppose looking back on it,

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.030
was probably in his
early to mid-20s

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:38.470
carrying his little daughter.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:41.560
And when we stopped
at that restaurant,

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:42.930
they would not serve him.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:46.040
And they would not
serve the little girl.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.130
And my mother decided that this
was not a reasonable thing.

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:53.290
And she had heard
what the man had

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:58.150
tried to order for his little
girl, which was a bowl of soup.

00:01:58.150 --> 00:02:02.050
So my mother ordered
an extra bowl of soup.

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:06.400
And the waitress of
course, did not refuse her.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.880
But before she handed the
bowl of soup to my mother,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:14.830
she reached over and just
laden it with pepper.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:19.990
And that electric
moment has just

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.970
always been with me ever since.

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:27.790
And I think that was the time at
which I lost some of my naiveté

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:34.430
about racial relations
in America and so forth.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.210
Looking in a more
pragmatic context,

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:41.810
and I don't think we should
be afraid to be pragmatic,

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.640
one also has to recognize
that our society has

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.810
the makeup that it has,
and we know perfectly well

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.830
within a time frame of
20 years or 30 years

00:02:54.830 --> 00:02:57.740
exactly what the racial and
ethnic makeup of this country

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:58.730
is.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:01.070
And on a pragmatic
scale, we simply

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:06.140
must learn to live, and
work, and advance together.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:11.070
The historical forces
make it a difficult task.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.300
The current economic forces make
it an even more daunting set

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.840
of problems.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.650
But if we are to be a
great nation in the future,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.950
somehow we have to resolve
and come to grips with it.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:27.020
So I think even in a
pragmatic sense, as well as

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.680
a moral sense, we, as a leading
institution of education

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.210
and research in the United
States in the 1990s,

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:40.580
must be dedicated to educating
increasing numbers of students

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:44.360
from the various components
of the American population.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:47.840
We must produce, from those
communities, the leaders

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.100
that the nation is going to need
downstream in 20 or 30 years.

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:57.230
So there will be no backing
off of this Institution's

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.200
commitment to these
matters despite the fact

00:04:00.200 --> 00:04:03.080
that we're in rather
difficult economic times,

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:07.540
it's got to be very,
very high on our agenda.

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:09.740
At the same time,
we must never be

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:14.060
naive about what is involved,
about the depth of effort that

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.290
is going to be required.

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:19.160
And as we do with any
student, we win these battles

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:22.130
and make our progression
one individual at a time

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.380
by individual's
commitments, by what

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.610
we can provide to individuals,
by what we can learn from them.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.880
And I hope as a
leader that I can

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:38.060
play at least some modest
part in inspiring and leading

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.670
our community on to undertake
this very important role.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:47.870
As you know, I have
seen the first video

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.720
that was made by our
African American students.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.110
And there was at least
one immediate lesson

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.720
in looking at that which
quite honestly surprised

00:04:56.720 --> 00:05:01.550
me, and that was the fact
that almost every one

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:04.790
of the students, in talking
about their personal

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:09.230
observations of racial
relations and so

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:12.680
forth on the campus from
their personal perspective,

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:18.860
immediately homed in on the
instructional aspects of MIT.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:22.670
Almost all of the
anecdotes that they

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:26.240
passed on to the rest of
us had to do with classroom

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:28.610
or other learning situations.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.070
That's very important
for us all to understand.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:33.260
It certainly is important
for me to understand

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:37.880
as an administrator and
a leader on the campus.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:40.460
The bad news is
it shows problems

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.810
in the most fundamentally
important aspect of what we do.

00:05:45.810 --> 00:05:47.600
But the good news
is that's something

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.940
that we can
consciously be aware of

00:05:49.940 --> 00:05:54.230
and try to work together
as a community to improve.

00:05:54.230 --> 00:05:58.640
So I see this as a wonderful
discovery and learning tool.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.580
And I'm very grateful to all
of you who are participating

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.230
in the project.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:11.740
You mentioned the question
of teaching diversity.

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:18.100
I find this a very difficult
concept to grapple with.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:24.310
I don't know if it is literally
possible to teach diversity

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:27.460
in the sense that one can
teach electronic circuits,

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:30.940
or calculus, or what have you.

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:38.980
My suspicion is that we
must accomplish education

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:41.860
about multiculturalism,
about diversity

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:44.980
in our society and
our activities,

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:46.780
on many different
planes and through

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:48.820
many different mechanisms.

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:53.080
Some, I know, would disagree
with this philosophy.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:59.020
But I find the subject to be so
broad, and so complex, and so

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:02.410
ill-defined that it
is not clear to me

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:05.770
that one can define
a single course,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.640
or a single curriculum,
that will satisfy

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:11.900
that need for all people.

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.640
So I think that we need
to think about diversity,

00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:20.440
multiculturalism, understanding
history, and sociology,

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:23.980
and technology, and other
subjects from a broad variety

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:27.190
of cultural and
racial perspectives

00:07:27.190 --> 00:07:28.580
in many different ways.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:30.350
I think we have
to do it formally.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.320
We have to do it informally.

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:36.040
This taping project is
a beautiful example.

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.130
Dr. Williams' course
is a good example.

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:42.020
Mel King's course
is a good example.

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.260
I think we have to do
this over and over again

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.420
in different venues
and different ways

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:50.230
to fit different
people and kind of just

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:53.320
build up an environment
where nobody gets

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:57.970
through the Institute
without some informal, if not

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:00.970
formal, exposure
to a disciplined

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:05.380
thinking about other
cultures, other races.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:07.160
There is no simple answer.

00:08:07.160 --> 00:08:09.880
If I knew it, I
certainly would share it

00:08:09.880 --> 00:08:12.220
and try to implement it.

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.130
But I think it's a question we
need to keep in front of us.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:17.470
We need to try a
lot of experiments.

00:08:17.470 --> 00:08:19.570
It needs to be part
of what we think

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:24.190
of as the hidden curriculum
at MIT in the same way

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:28.100
that professional ethics
and other things are.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:30.820
And I hope, over
time, that we can

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:34.340
make some progress together.

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:36.669
SIMONIDES: I'm an
old-timer at MIT.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.150
I'm the old man now.

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:41.419
But I still think of
myself as somebody

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:45.550
who is still exploring and
finding out about the culture.

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:50.620
And one of the things
that I'm finding out

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:54.050
is a series of
paradoxes that operate.

00:08:54.050 --> 00:08:59.570
You refer to MIT as a kind of
a arch place for technology,

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:03.170
and for science, and
for the inventive mind.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.210
And that's absolutely true.

00:09:05.210 --> 00:09:08.120
It is one of the
important notions

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:09.880
that people have about MIT.

00:09:09.880 --> 00:09:13.070
I have a little bit
different picture of MIT.

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:20.370
My picture is one where
there is like a soup

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:24.690
kettle with a lot of things,
different vegetables and so on,

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:30.780
are all bubbling up
and of a fermentation.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:34.320
And the given, in your
image, or in the metaphor

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.660
you made and mine, is brains.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:40.350
Because the truth is, every
time the MIT door opens,

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:44.820
brains come in, more brains, the
better brains, the new brains.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:46.990
And so that is a given.

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:52.170
But what happens
inside is really more

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:54.710
of this soup in
the cauldron than

00:09:54.710 --> 00:10:02.180
of a well-developed
and rational process.

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:07.220
And the question is, when the
door opens and brains come in,

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:11.650
also a lot of differences, a lot
of different elements come in--

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.900
we call it diversity and
we're very much for it,

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:16.340
everybody is these days--

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:20.020
and the question is, how do we
deal with it after it's here?

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:24.440
And how, when we desire it,
how do we bring it about

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.260
and how all that
works, and whether it

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:31.580
works to our satisfaction,
to our expectations.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.820
The job I have is an
equal opportunity officer

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.890
for the past 13
years, a long time.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:42.610
It's kind of the
official job of reporting

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:46.330
to the government about
statistics of diversity,

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.700
telling the world,
the country, how

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:52.750
we are doing on the issue
of integrating differences

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:55.100
in our community.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.270
And that job is a good job.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.020
That part of my
job is a good job.

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.030
MIT is doing well.

00:11:01.030 --> 00:11:04.240
We have every year in the
entering class of freshmen,

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.130
30% are Asian American.

00:11:06.130 --> 00:11:08.140
That's one of the
minorities in this country.

00:11:08.140 --> 00:11:11.820
We're doing very well by them.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:17.880
About 12%, 14% are what we call
under-represented minorities.

00:11:17.880 --> 00:11:20.390
We don't have nearly as many.

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:23.400
Here there are still
American minorities-- blacks,

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:29.250
Mexican Americans, Puerto Rican
Americans, Native Americans.

00:11:29.250 --> 00:11:31.860
And that's fine.

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:36.790
But when you look
from the inside,

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:40.540
I think the picture in
my job the past 13 years

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.570
is it is a job
full of frustration

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:48.940
because we don't anywhere
near succeed in this as

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.950
well as we've succeeded
in some other endeavors

00:11:50.950 --> 00:11:52.090
that MIT has taken.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:54.580
Why and how?

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:59.590
We have on the faculty
about 1,000 faculty members.

00:11:59.590 --> 00:12:03.420
And we have had something
like, at the highest number,

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:08.380
18 black faculty
members out of 1,000.

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:13.310
And that was about
14, 15 years ago.

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:15.770
We now have between 10 and 12.

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:18.330
So we have gone
backwards in that.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:20.550
Way back before,
in the '60s, we had

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.160
five freshmen, black
freshmen, in the freshman

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.842
class coming in.

00:12:24.842 --> 00:12:30.320
In '69, in one year,
we brought it up to 55.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:33.600
And this year, or the past
three or four years, we

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.370
have been hovering
between 60 and 80.

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:38.750
What we find is
that we have made

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:41.690
some gains, some dramatic
gains, very positive gains

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.530
in the past, and that we're
either stuck in some of them

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:47.780
or even gone backwards.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:49.945
And so that is a
picture of unsuccess.

00:12:49.945 --> 00:12:52.400
And when I talk about
the paradox here,

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:56.900
you have this great
fame and fortune making

00:12:56.900 --> 00:12:59.640
kind of society of young minds.

00:12:59.640 --> 00:13:02.150
And on the other hand,
you have this kind

00:13:02.150 --> 00:13:06.910
of unsuccess of not meeting
what we wish to meet.

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:08.540
The time honored
way, when you want

00:13:08.540 --> 00:13:11.780
to get something done
around here, first of all,

00:13:11.780 --> 00:13:15.160
you've got to know not to ask
because you may be told no.

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:18.520
It's easier to say I'm sorry
if you fail than to say

00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.760
may I. So what would
be the experiment

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:25.150
that we would start
here in the MIT spirit?

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.970
Well, we've got some
unstructured groups

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:30.880
of colleagues to work together
to talk about our differences.

00:13:30.880 --> 00:13:37.070
And we found out that it
was encouraging to people

00:13:37.070 --> 00:13:40.640
who came together, but it
was also a little bit weird,

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:43.280
if you will, a little
bit different to ask them

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:47.570
to sit down, maybe one-on-one,
pair them up on the first day,

00:13:47.570 --> 00:13:51.550
to talk about what's
different about them

00:13:51.550 --> 00:13:56.620
in order to make a bridge
to work together better.

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:01.540
Well, it was awkward,
but it was attractive.

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.970
Now why was it attractive?

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:06.790
I think it was because
our premise was probably

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:10.670
a right one, that when you
talk about your differences,

00:14:10.670 --> 00:14:13.460
you're talking about
yourself, you bring out,

00:14:13.460 --> 00:14:21.844
without much consideration, what
it is that you have deep down,

00:14:21.844 --> 00:14:23.510
if you will, perhaps
that you won't even

00:14:23.510 --> 00:14:25.740
admit makes you proud.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.595
The thing that you are yourself.

00:14:27.595 --> 00:14:32.480
And when you talk about
that, to make it legitimate,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:36.400
that you should talk about
that with a colleague at work

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:40.690
for the purpose of building a
relation, a connection, if you

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:42.420
will.

00:14:42.420 --> 00:14:44.810
It's a paradox because you're
talking about differences.

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:48.170
Differences lead people
to argue, to fight.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:50.150
People kill each
other on differences.

00:14:50.150 --> 00:14:52.970
You don't have to go very far
to read or learn about that

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:55.550
or even see it these days.

00:14:55.550 --> 00:15:00.380
And yet, the premise to
talk about differences,

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:04.580
to start with differences in
order to build on them so as

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:06.830
to make connections,
so it makes synergy

00:15:06.830 --> 00:15:07.940
and make working together.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:12.830
We hope that this
kind of program,

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:17.140
that this spirit, if you will,
of success in this environment

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:22.180
for minorities would
exude itself so

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:24.570
that the people that we bring
here, even for visitors,

00:15:24.570 --> 00:15:27.870
we will be able to entice.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:31.880
So now are their excuses?

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:33.230
There are a lot of excuses.

00:15:33.230 --> 00:15:37.700
When you don't succeed,
you turn to reasons.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:41.030
The reasonable people,
people with brains,

00:15:41.030 --> 00:15:42.935
are very good with
coming up with excuses.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:47.770
But still, the problem persists.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:50.380
And if you're somebody,
like Clarence or myself,

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:54.670
who has to be here and count and
one of the things you ask, you

00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:56.200
ask all our fellow
senior officers,

00:15:56.200 --> 00:16:01.820
we ask ourselves in our
own areas, every year,

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:04.880
how many appointments
will you make

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:09.710
on the faculty and the staff,
and so that we can talk.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:12.410
How many do you think
might be minorities?

00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:17.300
Well, for the past
decade of my 13 years,

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:22.720
the first 10, what we
got is a very reasonable,

00:16:22.720 --> 00:16:24.550
and a very honest
reply, that said,

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:28.650
I don't expect much
turnover in my areas.

00:16:28.650 --> 00:16:32.800
So one of the
ingenious, minor kind

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.140
of efforts that this
turned out to be important

00:16:35.140 --> 00:16:38.560
was for us to count, to
count ex post every year how

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.720
many new appointments we had.

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:42.130
And we surprised our colleagues.

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.410
We surprised ourselves
with the fact

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:46.330
that we were making
many more appointments

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:49.660
than we had expected we would
make because there's turnover.

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:52.360
I don't want to say people
die, but that happens too,

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.550
both people resign,
people leave.

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.230
And so we found
in the areas where

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:59.210
they expected to have
two or three turnover,

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:01.810
not just in the faculty, but
in the administration I'm

00:17:01.810 --> 00:17:03.760
talking about, now
when we found that they

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:06.230
had 12 in the past year.

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:09.140
And so one of the things
that we were able to do,

00:17:09.140 --> 00:17:13.089
and the reason I bring that up
is because that is the antidote

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:17.530
to excuse is the facts,
you just bring somebody

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:20.230
a piece of paper that
simply says, in your area,

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:22.510
there was so much
turnover last year.

00:17:22.510 --> 00:17:26.240
Now you tell us then.

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:32.590
We hired 46 new faculty
members last year.

00:17:32.590 --> 00:17:34.090
One of them was minority.

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:35.890
Is that reasonable?

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:39.250
I can stand up in front
of all of my friends, all

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.260
of my fellow senior
officers here,

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:45.580
and they will say, well,
that may be excusable,

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:47.500
but it is not very reasonable.

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:49.090
We want more.

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:52.640
So excuses exist.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:54.220
And they have very good reasons.

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:56.120
And for rational
people, they are very

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:58.490
rational and understandable.

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:02.640
But when they don't give
you the desired result,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:04.956
they're not good enough.

00:18:04.956 --> 00:18:12.770
KEYSER: It's my opinion that the
easiest emotion for us to have,

00:18:12.770 --> 00:18:19.320
the easier emotion, is
hate as opposed to love.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:21.390
Now it's not too hard
to see why it's easier

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:23.430
to hate than to love.

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:27.550
To love requires a
relationship with someone else.

00:18:27.550 --> 00:18:30.090
Hate, in fact, doesn't
require a relationship.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.940
In fact, it's much
easier to hate somebody

00:18:32.940 --> 00:18:35.400
that you don't know.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:38.500
What is one of the
properties of hatred?

00:18:38.500 --> 00:18:41.250
It seems to me that one of
the properties of hatred

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:48.800
is that it lowers our
unwillingness to act.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:52.820
Another way to put it
is, what hatred does

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:59.790
is make it far easier for
us to act on impulse rather

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.312
than rationally.

00:19:01.312 --> 00:19:05.490
Now let me give you
an example of why

00:19:05.490 --> 00:19:09.450
I believe that this is something
which is instinctual in us

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:13.080
and that we have to really
be constantly aware of.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.590
When somebody says blood,
we start to resonate.

00:19:16.590 --> 00:19:20.400
And we have to say, uh-oh,
that's our basic brain

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:23.700
resonating, let's not listen.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:27.690
At the very least, let's
get out of the room.

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.360
There was a famous
member of the faculty

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:34.860
here by the name of
Jerry Lettvin who

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:39.480
did work on what the frog's
eye tells the frog's brain.

00:19:39.480 --> 00:19:42.030
And Jerry discovered
that the frog actually

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:50.190
has five separate independent
neural detectors in its brain.

00:19:50.190 --> 00:19:54.450
Basically, these detectors,
each one does a separate thing.

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:57.000
There is a detector
that fires whenever

00:19:57.000 --> 00:20:00.420
an edge is introduced into
the frog's field of vision.

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:05.790
There is a detector that fires
whenever a small black object

00:20:05.790 --> 00:20:09.360
makes a trajectory toward the
center of an imaginary circle.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:15.480
There is a detector that
fires whenever light dims.

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:18.090
There's a detector
that fires whenever

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.990
there is blue in the vicinity.

00:20:21.990 --> 00:20:24.710
There is a detector
that fires whenever

00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:27.450
there is any change at all.

00:20:27.450 --> 00:20:32.340
These five detectors
are abstract properties

00:20:32.340 --> 00:20:35.490
which the frog has
extracted through evolution,

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:37.680
from the real world,
which has made it possible

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:40.590
for it to survive
virtually unchanged for two

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:42.270
million years.

00:20:42.270 --> 00:20:47.040
And I will show you why they're
so good at helping the frog

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:48.480
to survive.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:52.890
When a detector that sees an
edge and a small black dot

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:59.130
fires, the frog eats it because
it's a fly landing on a twig.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:05.340
Whenever the light dims,
the frog jumps towards blue.

00:21:05.340 --> 00:21:05.940
Why?

00:21:05.940 --> 00:21:07.680
Because the frog's
enemies attack

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.060
with the sun at its back.

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:10.950
And the closer the
enemy gets to the frog,

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.410
the less light gets
to the frog's eye.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:15.510
And what does he do when
he jumps towards blue?

00:21:15.510 --> 00:21:17.970
He jumps towards that part
of the world which reflects

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:21.385
the sky, which is water.

00:21:21.385 --> 00:21:24.120
Now what's this got
to do with hatred?

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:27.600
In my opinion, hatred
in human beings

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:31.020
is the event
detector in the frog.

00:21:31.020 --> 00:21:35.040
When anything ever happens,
the frog is suddenly alert.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:38.040
Any change at all, the
frog is suddenly alert.

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.550
And what suddenly
alert means is,

00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:43.470
I'm ready to act
instantaneously,

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:45.640
without thought.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.290
And I believe that
in human beings,

00:21:47.290 --> 00:21:52.350
hatred is an event detector
which says, I'm ready to act.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:55.652
And tribalism is
jumping towards blue.

00:21:55.652 --> 00:21:57.730
PARKER: What do
we have to bridge?

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:01.240
And based on that
comment, it was explicit

00:22:01.240 --> 00:22:03.970
what we have to bridge, how
to understand other people

00:22:03.970 --> 00:22:06.310
cultures, and how
to respect them,

00:22:06.310 --> 00:22:10.540
and how can we come together and
synergize all of our energies

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:13.900
as a people, as
Americans, no matter

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:16.420
whether you're Italian
American, African American,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:20.330
Chinese American, how can we
take that and become a better

00:22:20.330 --> 00:22:20.830
America?

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:24.950
So the response was, we
don't know what to do.

00:22:24.950 --> 00:22:26.260
How do we start this?

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:29.650
But the best way to do that is,
how do you define yourselves,

00:22:29.650 --> 00:22:31.870
and how do other people
define themselves,

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:33.972
and how can we deal with that?

00:22:33.972 --> 00:22:36.940
The central part
of the class is not

00:22:36.940 --> 00:22:39.580
how much material
you cover, it's

00:22:39.580 --> 00:22:43.180
how much conversation
you stimulate.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:46.270
Because once people start
talking, they open up

00:22:46.270 --> 00:22:48.260
and you are able to
understand how they think.

00:22:48.260 --> 00:22:51.580
And I think bridging
the gap, the key element

00:22:51.580 --> 00:22:55.990
is how other people think,
not what topics are covered.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:59.190
This type of teaching
should be a staple.

00:22:59.190 --> 00:23:02.100
Everybody should have some
multi-ethnic, multicultural

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:05.090
training because that's
what tomorrow is.

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:06.630
And that's what today is.

00:23:06.630 --> 00:23:09.490
And I don't think people
are properly prepared.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:12.030
CHUN: I'm sure I've built
my own boundaries, in a way,

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:17.510
as a biology major, as I
don't know, as being Korean,

00:23:17.510 --> 00:23:19.230
and whether just
staying with Koreans,

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:21.120
if I just take a
look at my friends

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:25.700
and I happen to notice
that they're all Korean,

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:29.790
it's provided just a medium for
me to communicate with others

00:23:29.790 --> 00:23:32.160
and to realize that,
yes, we have differences,

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:34.410
we can bring our unique
differences together,

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.010
and yet we can get along.

00:23:36.010 --> 00:23:38.520
And so I'm grateful for that.

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.640
And it's just the beginning
of this door opening.

00:23:41.640 --> 00:23:45.360
And hopefully, we
talked about how

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:47.190
we're going to be
agents of change

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:53.275
and how we can get a
chain reaction going.

00:23:53.275 --> 00:23:59.270
WILLIAMS: About three years
ago, it became very clear to me

00:23:59.270 --> 00:24:03.230
that the student
body, particularly

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:09.740
the undergraduate student
body, was so visibly changing

00:24:09.740 --> 00:24:14.760
in terms of the
students of color.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:19.850
And I think that it was
perhaps maybe the first year

00:24:19.850 --> 00:24:29.540
that we had a freshman class
that consisted of more than 50%

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:32.525
of students of color and
international students.

00:24:35.690 --> 00:24:43.090
And at that time, I did not feel
that we were taking advantage

00:24:43.090 --> 00:24:51.805
of this rich culture that
existed, racial and ethnicity,

00:24:51.805 --> 00:24:54.280
I would say, here.

00:24:54.280 --> 00:24:57.340
And so therefore, I thought that
it would be a very good idea

00:24:57.340 --> 00:25:02.770
to begin to give a
seminar based on the fact

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:07.000
that the large
number of students

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.640
who were on undergraduate
level did not

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:13.760
appear to me to be communicating
at all with each other.

00:25:13.760 --> 00:25:21.140
I'll tell you basically what the
objectives of the seminar are.

00:25:21.140 --> 00:25:25.070
And I think that I
have to preface this

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:30.140
by saying that one
of the real issues,

00:25:30.140 --> 00:25:35.420
I believe, particularly
in the year 2000,

00:25:35.420 --> 00:25:40.300
is the fact that our
young men and women who

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:43.340
will go into the
workforce will have

00:25:43.340 --> 00:25:51.470
to learn how to deal with
more women in the workforce

00:25:51.470 --> 00:25:57.950
and deal more with the new
workers in the workforce.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:01.400
I think that minorities will
constitute about one third

00:26:01.400 --> 00:26:04.190
of the workforce
in the year 2000.

00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:06.710
And also, the
companies, in order

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:09.800
to be internationally
competitive,

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:12.140
are going to have
to be able to deal

00:26:12.140 --> 00:26:14.150
with the whole
issue of teamwork,

00:26:14.150 --> 00:26:17.390
regardless of who people
are, whether they are handy,

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:20.420
whether they are people
with disabilities,

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:23.240
whether they are women,
whether they are minorities.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:28.700
So I say all that to say that
the objectives of the course

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:33.530
really is to try to help young
men and women here at MIT

00:26:33.530 --> 00:26:37.460
to begin to get ready to
deal with the workforce

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:38.630
in that relationship.

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:46.920
What I really would like to see
is an Institute developed here

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:51.240
at the Institute,
at MIT, that will

00:26:51.240 --> 00:26:58.710
do more than just the
course, but be an agency that

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:03.210
will provide lots
of kinds of support

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:09.240
for helping not only students,
but faculty who I think need

00:27:09.240 --> 00:27:12.180
a lot of help in understanding
this racial and cultural

00:27:12.180 --> 00:27:16.270
differences that
will not go away,

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:19.420
but will increase substantially.

00:27:19.420 --> 00:27:22.590
So I think we need a lot
of help to understand

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:27.570
some of the differences that
exist through our students

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:29.835
and hopefully, our faculty.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.920
I'm not sure that equal
opportunity is the right name

00:27:34.920 --> 00:27:35.880
anymore.

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:37.770
I'm not even sure that
affirmative action

00:27:37.770 --> 00:27:39.240
is the right name.

00:27:39.240 --> 00:27:44.310
I think that we need to
look at the whole structure

00:27:44.310 --> 00:27:50.430
of this whole effort
and find a better

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:54.300
way of being able to
bring people of color,

00:27:54.300 --> 00:27:56.970
and people who
have disabilities,

00:27:56.970 --> 00:28:03.450
women back together
more as a team.

00:28:03.450 --> 00:28:06.060
And it requires some
other kinds of things

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:12.120
other than talking about the
affirmative action aspect.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:14.310
I think the numbers are
going to take care of itself

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:17.250
if you can get people
understanding that it has

00:28:17.250 --> 00:28:19.980
nothing to do with
the issue of color,

00:28:19.980 --> 00:28:22.950
or gender, and some of
the other stereotypes

00:28:22.950 --> 00:28:24.570
that I think we have.

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:26.070
And I think that
we need to put more

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:27.810
effort on some other things.

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:30.583
And I think that will
make a difference.

00:28:30.583 --> 00:28:32.958
[MUSIC - STEVIE WONDER, "PASTIME
 PARADISE"] They've been

00:28:32.958 --> 00:28:38.381
spending most their lives
living in a future paradise.

00:28:38.381 --> 00:28:40.846
They've been spending
most their lives

00:28:40.846 --> 00:28:44.297
living in a future paradise.

00:28:44.297 --> 00:28:47.620
They've been looking in
their minds for the day

00:28:47.620 --> 00:28:50.090
that sorrows lost from time.

00:28:50.090 --> 00:28:54.460
They keep telling of the
day when the savior of love

00:28:54.460 --> 00:28:55.720
will come to stay.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:29:01.694
Tell me who of them
will come to be.

00:29:01.694 --> 00:29:07.390
How many of them are you and me.

00:29:07.390 --> 00:29:10.075
Proclamation.

00:29:10.075 --> 00:29:13.875
Of race relations.

00:29:13.875 --> 00:29:16.698
Consolation.

00:29:16.698 --> 00:29:19.680
Integration.

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:21.452
Verification.

00:29:21.452 --> 00:29:23.200
Of revelations.

00:29:23.200 --> 00:29:24.760
Acclamation.

00:29:24.760 --> 00:29:26.170
World salvation.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:27.670
Vibrations.

00:29:27.670 --> 00:29:29.170
Stimulation.

00:29:29.170 --> 00:29:33.420
Confirmation, to the
peace of the world.

